<%# THE STRIP'S ONE ADD (Shop manual spec §7; design-system.md rules 8 and 9). It holds what the top
    bar's Upload and New ▾ held: Upload…, a new folder, a text file, a project, the tools that make
    something, and an inventory item. Everything it makes lands in FolderDestination's folder, set by
    the action that renders a folder (the Library, a file's page, a project hub, a tool page) and by
    nothing else; a page with none asks with the folder chooser before the file picker opens
    (upload_pick_controller.js), and New folder and Text file go to the library root there.

    THE UPLOAD MACHINERY STAYS OUTSIDE THE MENU, in `.topbar-upload` (named for the top bar it was
    built in): the hidden input, the hidden Upload BUTTON, and the chunk flow's `.upload-status`,
    which must stay visible while an upload runs, and a closed <details> hides everything but its
    summary. The menu's "Upload…" clicks that BUTTON (upload_forward_controller.js#open), never the
    file input: on a page with no folder the button's click is what asks for the folder first, and
    an input-targeted click would upload to the library root unasked.

    ONE BUTTON, TWO FLOWS — the Files page's own, moved to the top bar in slice 4 and here unchanged.
    `port_writable?` picks WHICH DOOR the bytes go in (CLAUDE.md §14a — it is not "can this
    deployment upload"; rendering the wrong one ships a button whose door answers 501), and both
    carry the destination as the `parent_node_id` the doors already accept.

    A ZIP HOLDING A MODEL FILE UNPACKS (2026-10-21; docs/superpowers/specs/
    2026-10-20-zip-upload-unpacks-models-design.md): both flows carry the two unpack doors and the
    server's model extensions, and lib/zip_unpack.js does the rest in the browser.

    UPLOAD IS A <button> (UX audit I, "Unfocusable primary action"). It was a <label> over the hidden
    file input, which takes no focus, so a keyboard could not upload at all. The input is the
    button's SIBLING (an input inside a button is invalid), and a click on the button opens it: on a
    page with a folder through upload_forward_controller.js#choose, on a page with none through
    upload_pick_controller.js#pick, which asks for the folder first. The keyboard reaches it through
    the menu's "Upload…", itself a button.

    Add is an outlined button on every page: the strip spends no accent, and Upload no longer carries
    one (it was the Library's, Recent's and the hub's primary while it sat in the top bar). %>
<% dest = folder_destination %>
<% parent_value = dest ? dest.node_id.to_s : "" %>
<% new_parent = new_menu_folder_node_id %>
<% pick = dest.nil? %>
<% btn_title = dest ? "Saves to #{dest.caption}" : "Choose a folder, then files" %>
<% btn_data = pick ? { action: "click->upload-pick#pick" } : { controller: "upload-forward", action: "upload-forward#choose" } %>
<%# The chooser's controller sits on the wrapper of BOTH halves, so its caption target can live in the
    menu while its input and parent targets live in `.topbar-upload`. %>
<%= tag.div class: "strip-add",
            data: (pick ? { controller: "upload-pick",
                            upload_pick_children_url_value: library_children_path,
                            upload_pick_create_url_value: library_folders_path } : {}) do %>
  <details class="menu strip-add-menu" data-controller="menu">
    <summary class="btn strip-add-btn" aria-label="Add"><%= rail_icon(:plus) %><span class="strip-add-label">Add</span></summary>
    <div class="menu-list">
      <%# WHERE IT ALL LANDS, said once at the head of the menu. On a page with no folder the chooser
          writes it here after a choice (upload_pick_controller.js), and until then it is hidden. %>
      <% if dest %>
        <p class="topbar-upload-caption">Saves to <strong><%= dest.caption %></strong></p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="topbar-upload-caption" data-upload-pick-target="caption" hidden></p>
      <% end %>
      <button type="button" class="menu-item strip-add-upload" data-controller="upload-forward"
              data-action="upload-forward#open">Upload…</button>
      <div class="menu-sep" role="separator"></div>
      <%= form_with url: library_folders_path, method: :post, class: "menu-form", data: { turbo: false } do %>
        <%# `.to_s`: no folder is the library root, sent as "" exactly as Upload sends it. %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag :parent_node_id, new_parent.to_s, id: nil %>
        <label class="menu-form-label" for="new_folder_name">New folder</label>
        <span class="menu-form-row">
          <%= text_field_tag :name, nil, id: "new_folder_name", placeholder: "Folder name",
                             required: true, autocomplete: "off" %>
          <%= submit_tag "Create", class: "small", name: nil %>
        </span>
      <% end %>
      <div class="menu-sep" role="separator"></div>
      <%= link_to "Text file", new_files_text_file_path(parent_node_id: new_parent), class: "menu-item" %>
      <%= link_to "Project", new_project_path, class: "menu-item" %>
      <%# THE TOOL ENTRIES ARRIVE ONLY WHEN THE MENU OPENS. A lazy frame inside a closed <details> has
          no box, so Turbo's IntersectionObserver fires on open and not before: the layout itself never
          reads the addon registry (docs/invariants/addons.md), and an addon-source outage costs this
          menu its tool list instead of costing every page its render. `target="_top"` so a tool link
          navigates the page, not the frame. %>
      <turbo-frame id="new-menu-tools" src="<%= tools_new_menu_path(in: new_parent) %>" loading="lazy" target="_top"></turbo-frame>
      <div class="menu-sep" role="separator"></div>
      <%= link_to "Inventory item", new_inventory_item_path, class: "menu-item" %>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="topbar-upload">
    <% if capabilities.port_writable? %>
      <%# Share/S3: no chunk store to presign into, so the bytes come up in one multipart POST. %>
      <%# THE ZIP-UPLOAD CONTROLLER (2026-10-21) submits this form exactly as the inline `onchange` it
          replaced did, for every selection without a model zip; a selection holding one is unpacked
          in the browser instead (zip_upload_controller.js, lib/zip_unpack.js). %>
      <%= form_with url: files_path, method: :post, class: "uploader",
                    data: { turbo: false, controller: "zip-upload",
                            zip_upload_create_url_value: files_path,
                            zip_upload_unpack_url_value: files_unpack_folder_path,
                            zip_upload_unpacked_url_value: files_unpacked_path,
                            zip_upload_model_exts_value: Maker::FileAttachment::MODEL_EXTS },
                    html: { multipart: true } do |f| %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag :parent_node_id, parent_value, id: nil,
                             data: { zip_upload_target: "parent", upload_pick_target: ("parent" if pick) } %>
        <%# THE WIZARD'S WAY BACK (2026-11-09): only WelcomeController#files sets upload_return, so every
            other page renders no field and files#create redirects as it always has. %>
        <% if upload_return %>
          <%= hidden_field_tag :return_to, upload_return.flag, id: nil %>
        <% end %>
        <%# `include_hidden: false` IS LOAD-BEARING. `f.file_field` with `multiple: true` renders a
            companion `<input name="file[]" type="hidden" value="">` so an empty selection still
            submits the key, which put a bare "" at the head of `params[:file]`, and the server
            called `original_filename` on a String and 500'd. Nothing needs that marker here: the
            field's own change handler is the only thing that submits this form, so it never fires
            with an empty selection. Found by uploading four real files through the real form; the
            Rack::Test suites POSTed a Ruby array and never sent the marker at all, which is §14d's
            "assert the rendered field name" with a different attribute (upload_batch_test.rb). %>
        <%= f.file_field :file, multiple: true, include_hidden: false, id: "topbar_upload_file", hidden: true,
                         data: { zip_upload_target: "input", action: "change->zip-upload#change",
                                 upload_pick_target: ("input" if pick) } %>
        <%= tag.button type: "button", class: "upload-btn", title: btn_title, data: btn_data do %>
          <span>Upload</span>
        <% end %>
        <span class="upload-status" data-zip-upload-target="status"></span>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <%# Chunk: the browser hashes, we presign the missing chunks, the browser PUTs them (§15). The
          section is built with `tag.section` so its optional `uploader` target is an attribute the
          builder writes, never a string spliced into an open tag. %>
      <%= tag.section class: "uploader",
                      data: { controller: "uploader",
                              uploader_begin_url_value: begin_upload_files_path,
                              uploader_finalize_url_value: finalize_upload_files_path,
                              uploader_parent_node_id_value: parent_value,
                              uploader_unpack_url_value: files_unpack_folder_path,
                              uploader_unpacked_url_value: files_unpacked_path,
                              uploader_model_exts_value: Maker::FileAttachment::MODEL_EXTS,
                              uploader_redirect_url_value: upload_return&.url,
                              upload_pick_target: ("uploader" if pick) } do %>
        <%= tag.input type: "file", multiple: true, id: "topbar_upload_file", hidden: true,
                      data: { uploader_target: "input", action: "uploader#upload",
                              upload_pick_target: ("input" if pick) } %>
        <%= tag.button type: "button", class: "upload-btn", title: btn_title, data: btn_data do %>
          <span>Upload</span>
        <% end %>
        <span class="upload-status" data-uploader-target="status"></span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
